WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.488 --> 00:00:04.321
[screaming]

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:10.410
Narrator: That scream you're hearing

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.330
in this one moment from "Venom"?

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.180
That's not Tom Hardy.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:15.700
That's Scott Whyte.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.494
Scream artists are some of
Hollywood's best-kept secrets.

00:00:19.494 --> 00:00:21.510
Like, actually secret.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:22.779
Take Ashley Peldon.

00:00:22.779 --> 00:00:24.980
[fearful scream]

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.460
She's one of the most accomplished
screamers in Hollywood,

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:30.200
but you might not know where to find her,

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.844
as productions don't always
want audiences to know

00:00:32.844 --> 00:00:34.832
that their stars have been dubbed.

00:00:34.832 --> 00:00:38.460
[screaming] No!

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.710
Narrator: This makes
nailing both the emotion

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:44.461
and the actor's voice
that much more important.

00:00:44.461 --> 00:00:46.530
Here's how they get the perfect ...

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:47.590
[screams]

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:49.758
I'm sure I blasted you guys.

00:00:49.758 --> 00:00:51.520
Narrator: A lot of the
sounds captured on set

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:52.780
aren't actually usable,

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:54.910
so they have to be rerecorded later.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:56.220
Peldon: They great background actors

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.540
who were there on set that
day probably weren't miked,

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.417
or maybe there was too
much going on in the noise.

00:01:00.417 --> 00:01:02.530
They'll have wind machines
going and sound effects,

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.180
or you'll have the
stuntman or the director

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:05.220
yelling at the actors.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:06.550
Narrator: But when it comes to screams,

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.280
sound quality is just one factor.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.320
Screams are physically difficult.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.088
Just look at this clip of Hugh Jackman

00:01:13.088 --> 00:01:15.030
dubbing some of his noises from "Logan."

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:18.363
[grunting and huffing]

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.470
So if an actor wants to
keep their voice intact,

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:26.052
these skilled recording artists can help.

00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:27.570
You know, maybe I'm more expendable,

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:29.907
'cause for a session fee
I can blow out my voice.

00:01:29.907 --> 00:01:31.680
Narrator: Peldon and
Whyte first need to know

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.370
what type of scream they're doing,

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:35.370
as that will impact their performance.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.730
One of the most common
screams you'll see in movies

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:41.347
are fearful ones, but they're
not actually realistic.

00:01:41.347 --> 00:01:42.802
Peldon: Now, what's
interesting is in real life,

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:45.055
most people will probably freeze up

00:01:45.055 --> 00:01:46.537
and not a lot of sound comes out,

00:01:46.537 --> 00:01:48.080
but it's not as dramatic on screen.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.696
So we want to make sure that it's a big,

00:01:49.696 --> 00:01:51.740
loud, terrifying scream.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:54.420
Narrator: Determining where
in the body fear comes from

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.550
can make the scream more believable.

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.650
Peldon says fear screams
should feel involuntary,

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:00.690
even if they aren't.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.656
So she puts her diaphragm to work.

00:02:02.656 --> 00:02:03.990
Peldon: You want it as messy as possible

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.368
to get it the most realistic.

00:02:05.368 --> 00:02:07.487
So, like, if you hear just a [screams],

00:02:07.487 --> 00:02:11.470
you know, really clean, short,
or contained kind of scream,

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:13.300
it's not really as realistic

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.420
for somebody who's in a lot of fear.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.670
But if somebody is fearing for their life

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.890
or in a really terrifying situation,

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:21.530
it's got the adrenaline pumping,

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.210
it's got the sweat, it's got the movement.

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:25.340
So it's going to be more
of a [terrified scream]

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:26.778
kind of a feel scream.

00:02:26.778 --> 00:02:28.640
Narrator: You can see and hear this

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.810
as Peldon watches an old horror clip.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:32.618
Peldon: Where it's the building tension

00:02:32.618 --> 00:02:33.760
is what's important there.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:36.935
This one to me would feel more of a fear

00:02:36.935 --> 00:02:38.501
[gasps and screams],

00:02:38.501 --> 00:02:41.190
building kind of scream
rather than a [screams],

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:42.720
you know, blood-curdling scream.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.790
Narrator: But what makes that scream ...

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.600
[fearful scream]

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.482
sound different from this one?

00:02:49.482 --> 00:02:51.320
[screaming in rage]

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.990
Peldon: Rage is the
emotion of anger, right?

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:57.732
So it's screaming with the
intention of pent-up emotions.

00:02:57.732 --> 00:02:59.070
You need to let it out.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.100
Narrator: Screams that
come from a place of anger

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.210
call for a clenched mouth
and tighter body position.

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:06.190
And a more empowering rage scream

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:08.737
calls for Peldon to fully open her mouth.

00:03:09.901 --> 00:03:11.357
[screaming in rage]

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:13.548
Whyte will do the same
when recording war cries

00:03:13.548 --> 00:03:15.231
for games like Call of Duty.

00:03:15.231 --> 00:03:19.298
[screaming in rage]

00:03:19.298 --> 00:03:21.640
For other roles, Peldon
will channel grief.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:25.960
So, grief has a different feel
because this is pure pain,

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:27.360
but not physical pain, right?

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.280
This is just emotional pain.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.300
I tend to think of this
as the most uncontained

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.940
and unhinged kind of feeling.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:35.020
Narrator: When syncing to a clip,

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:37.730
she'll often need to
give multiple options.

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:40.008
This is a "no" she's saying.

00:03:40.008 --> 00:03:43.112
Narrator: Listen as she tries
one scream with the word "no."

00:03:43.112 --> 00:03:46.645
No!

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.183
Narrator: And one without.

00:03:50.183 --> 00:03:53.791
[screaming in grief]

00:03:55.466 --> 00:03:57.520
Convincing screams involve much more

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.988
than just incorporating dialogue.

00:03:59.988 --> 00:04:03.017
Darth Vader: No!

00:04:03.017 --> 00:04:05.192
Narrator: Listen to all the extra pants

00:04:05.192 --> 00:04:07.283
and breaths Whyte adds to his screams

00:04:07.283 --> 00:04:10.228
during a fight scene from
the video game Days Gone.

00:04:10.228 --> 00:04:11.755
[screams]

00:04:11.755 --> 00:04:15.422
[huffing and panting]

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:20.900
These wordless physical
reactions are called efforts,

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:22.750
and they're crucial to fight screams.

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:24.580
It's different then when
it's a scream of fear.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:26.450
That's more of a pull-in sensation.

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:29.165
Usually these are more of a
push-out kind of sensation.

00:04:29.165 --> 00:04:30.830
There's an impact attached.

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:33.390
Narrator: Each breath and grunt
will have subtle differences

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.750
based on the type of distress
their characters experience.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.817
A gut shot would knock
the wind out of you,

00:04:39.817 --> 00:04:42.700
which is more of a [huffs].

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:45.002
Face punch would be
more of a quick strike,

00:04:45.002 --> 00:04:47.550
like [grunting].

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:48.684
Narrator: While this effort ...

00:04:48.684 --> 00:04:50.300
[grunts and shouts]

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:53.425
fits in perfectly when White's
character delivers a kick,

00:04:53.425 --> 00:04:54.334
this one ...

00:04:54.334 --> 00:04:56.520
[grunts]

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.980
matches when he swings a machete.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:01.740
All these variations help
keep the scene alive.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:05.140
All of the energy when the
camera's not on my character

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:07.387
to keep that character
going in the background.

00:05:07.387 --> 00:05:09.153
Narrator: But some
screams don't fit neatly

00:05:09.153 --> 00:05:10.669
into one category.

00:05:10.669 --> 00:05:12.430
For example, in "Incredibles 2,"

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:14.780
Whyte had to create the scream for a pilot

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:16.700
who's thrown out of a helicopter.

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:19.390
Whyte: The fear of being
pushed out of the helicopter,

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:21.085
which then turns into anger, like,

00:05:21.085 --> 00:05:23.610
"Ah! I shouldn't have been
kicked out of that plane!"

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:24.459
You know, or whatever.

00:05:24.459 --> 00:05:27.559
So it couldn't just be a fear [screams]

00:05:27.559 --> 00:05:28.475
and then you hit the water.

00:05:28.475 --> 00:05:30.288
It had to have a little two-part act.

00:05:30.288 --> 00:05:31.980
And once you hit water,

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.690
you're not going to hear
any more of the "ah!"

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:35.966
You don't hear the arc of the voice.

00:05:35.966 --> 00:05:37.507
It's just [cuts off scream].

00:05:38.724 --> 00:05:41.733
[cartoonish scream]

00:05:41.733 --> 00:05:43.420
Narrator: This process gets even trickier

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:46.654
when scream artists dub
for a high-profile actor,

00:05:46.654 --> 00:05:48.121
like Whyte did for Tom Hardy

00:05:48.121 --> 00:05:50.330
during the motorcycle chase in "Venom."

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:52.630
Whyte first needed to
study the actor's voice

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:54.653
to figure out what makes it unique.

00:05:56.192 --> 00:05:59.140
So, Tom Hardy has sort of a gruff,

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:01.050
sort of a lower sort of a voice thing.

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:03.241
So I knew going in that it had to have

00:06:03.241 --> 00:06:04.730
a little more grit to the voice.

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:07.250
Narrator: Next, he did what's
called "chasing the scene,"

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:09.527
where he watches the sequence
from start to finish,

00:06:09.527 --> 00:06:12.527
adding all screams and efforts
as the scene progresses.

00:06:12.527 --> 00:06:14.135
[screams]

00:06:14.135 --> 00:06:17.186
[huffing]

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:22.935
Cut two. Now this is when you breathe

00:06:22.935 --> 00:06:24.310
and don't pass out.

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:26.575
Feeling a little light-headed,
but you push through.

00:06:26.575 --> 00:06:27.904
Back to it.

00:06:27.904 --> 00:06:29.120
[grunts]

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.700
And you can tell it's a workout.

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:32.430
One short few-minute scene in,

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:34.030
and I'm already sweating bullets.

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:36.940
Narrator: After that breather,
Whyte looked for specifics.

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:39.680
We're really trying to hone
in on the actual scream itself

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:41.070
and analyze the scream.

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:43.649
Narrator: Like when an
explosion goes off behind Hardy.

00:06:43.649 --> 00:06:46.240
Whyte: He's got his closed
mouth, and his teeth are showing.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:47.990
And so you have to yell through your teeth

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:49.980
to match that performance.

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:52.615
Narrator: Then, when Hardy
flies off his motorcycle,

00:06:52.615 --> 00:06:55.590
Whyte had to sustain a
scream for nearly 10 seconds

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:58.410
without losing the
imitation of Hardy's voice.

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:00.974
The art of a long scream is to kind of

00:07:00.974 --> 00:07:03.200
tighten your vocal folds just enough

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:04.888
and release just enough air

00:07:04.888 --> 00:07:07.821
that you can prolong that
scream as long as people need.

00:07:07.821 --> 00:07:10.800
When you do that, you're kind
of tightening your stomach.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:15.610
While, again, Tom Hardy's
voice is more grittier, lower.

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:16.720
So you kind of have to get

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:19.330
that gravelly thing in your scream

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:22.570
while you're pinching your vocal folds

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:25.026
and tightening your diaphragm
just to get out enough air.

00:07:25.026 --> 00:07:26.869
'Cause if you're pushing too much air,

00:07:26.869 --> 00:07:28.261
you're going to run out of air.

00:07:28.261 --> 00:07:30.344
[screams]

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:40.081
Narrator: All of these screams,

00:07:40.081 --> 00:07:42.730
whether they're fear,
rage, or fighting sounds,

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:44.650
won't carry the same emotional punch

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:47.310
until they've gone through
a layering process.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:49.961
Those layers can include
the actor's real vocals

00:07:49.961 --> 00:07:52.740
mixed with the dubbed
vocals and digital sounds.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:55.891
Layering is especially
helpful for nonhuman voices,

00:07:55.891 --> 00:07:58.420
like when Whyte performed
the otherworldly screams

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:01.856
of the demon from "The Conjuring:
The Devil Made Me Do It."

00:08:01.856 --> 00:08:04.490
[screaming]

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:05.410
Here's a demonstration

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:08.035
of how a sound mixer might
take Whyte's raw screams

00:08:08.035 --> 00:08:11.012
and add effects to make
them more powerful.

00:08:11.012 --> 00:08:14.254
[growling]

00:08:15.248 --> 00:08:17.748
[screaming while inhaling]

00:08:18.865 --> 00:08:21.698
[breathy growling]

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:26.316
Listen to these three
elements combined in a scene.

00:08:26.316 --> 00:08:29.433
[growling scream]

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:31.640
Creepy, right?

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:34.252
Well, adding effects like
reverb to create an echo

00:08:34.252 --> 00:08:36.525
and a rumble can make them even scarier.

00:08:36.525 --> 00:08:39.442
[echoing growling scream]

00:08:41.094 --> 00:08:43.747
You know, using what the human body can do

00:08:43.747 --> 00:08:46.126
and then taking effects,

00:08:46.126 --> 00:08:47.558
putting it all in the melting pot.

00:08:47.558 --> 00:08:49.791
Narrator: These clips
don't seem as exciting,

00:08:49.791 --> 00:08:51.432
frightening, or sad

00:08:51.432 --> 00:08:54.460
until a scream artist's
work is added to it.

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:58.052
[Whyte screaming]

00:08:58.052 --> 00:09:00.711
[Peldon screaming in horror]

00:09:00.711 --> 00:09:04.259
[Peldon screaming in grief]

00:09:04.259 --> 00:09:06.759
So, what makes one of
Peldon's real screams ...

00:09:06.759 --> 00:09:08.680
[cheerful scream]

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:10.242
sound better than this one?

00:09:10.242 --> 00:09:12.160
[crowd cheering]

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:14.172
I think what makes the difference between

00:09:14.172 --> 00:09:16.210
what sounds kind of like a generic scream

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:18.320
that they could just get
from their library of screams

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:20.300
versus one that really fits the scene

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:22.033
is matching what we're seeing.

00:09:22.033 --> 00:09:23.908
The actor, the energy, the emotion.

00:09:23.908 --> 00:09:27.742
My scream isn't just noise;
it's an acting burst.

00:09:27.742 --> 00:09:29.764
"Six screams, two seconds
each, go. Give us options."

00:09:29.764 --> 00:09:30.597
And you're like,

00:09:30.597 --> 00:09:35.597
[screaming]

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:38.890
You know, and then they'll
grab each of those files

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:41.145
and use them all in different
places of the game, so.

